


\section*{Temporal Clustering Features for Double Faults}

We model double fault (DF) events as temporally dependent occurrences within a match sequence. To quantify clustering behavior, we construct multi-scale lag features across point, game, and set levels.

\subsection*{Point-Level Lag}
\begin{equation}
L_p(t) = p_t - p_{t-1}^{DF}
\end{equation}
where $p_t$ is the current point number and $p_{t-1}^{DF}$ is the point number of the most recent double fault prior to time $t$.

\subsection*{Game-Level Lag}
\begin{equation}
L_g(t) = g_t - g_{t-1}^{DF}
\end{equation}
where $g_t$ denotes the current game number and $g_{t-1}^{DF}$ is the game number of the last double fault event.

\subsection*{Set-Level Lag}
\begin{equation}
L_s(t) = s_t - s_{t-1}^{DF}
\end{equation}
where $s_t$ represents the current set and $s_{t-1}^{DF}$ is the set in which the previous double fault occurred.

\subsection*{Double Fault Indicator}
\begin{equation}
D(t) =
\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if a double fault occurs at time } t \\
0, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\subsection*{Clustering Intensity Score}
To quantify local clustering of double faults, we define:
\begin{equation}
C(t) = \frac{1}{1 + L_p(t)}
\end{equation}
where higher values indicate stronger temporal clustering of double faults.

\subsection*{Rolling Double Fault Pressure}
We define a rolling window measure of service instability:
\begin{equation}
P(t) = \sum_{i=t-k}^{t} D(i)
\end{equation}
where $k$ is the window size over recent points.



