\section*{Temporal Clustering of Double Faults}

We model double fault (DF) events as temporally dependent occurrences within a match sequence. To capture instability in serving behavior, we construct multi-scale lag features across point, game, and set levels.

\subsection*{Temporal Lag Features}

\begin{equation}
L_p(t) = p_t - p_{t-1}^{DF}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
L_g(t) = g_t - g_{t-1}^{DF}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
L_s(t) = s_t - s_{t-1}^{DF}
\end{equation}

where $p_t$, $g_t$, and $s_t$ represent the current point, game, and set indices respectively, and $t-1^{DF}$ denotes the most recent prior double fault event at each level.

\subsection*{Double Fault Indicator}

\begin{equation}
D(t) =
\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if a double fault occurs at time } t \\
0, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\subsection*{Clustering Intensity}

\begin{equation}
C(t) = \frac{1}{1 + L_p(t)}
\end{equation}

This score captures local temporal clustering of double faults, where smaller lag values indicate higher instability.

\subsection*{Rolling Pressure Metric}

\begin{equation}
P(t) = \sum_{i=t-k}^{t} D(i)
\end{equation}

where $k$ defines a rolling window over recent points.

\subsection*{Interpretation}

This formulation transforms raw match events into a hierarchical temporal process, enabling the detection of:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Micro-level clustering (point spacing)
    \item Meso-level structure (game-level instability)
    \item Macro-level trends (set-level breakdowns)
\end{itemize}

